{"language": "en", "segments": [{"start": 0.0, "end": 5.96, "text": "In 1988, a man who weighed 60 kilos lifted 190 kilos over his head,"}, {"start": 6.46, "end": 12.12, "text": "more than three times his own body weight. They called him Pocket Hercules. It's the anime trope"}, {"start": 12.12, "end": 16.88, "text": "made real. The little guy who shouldn't be strong, and then he lifts something ridiculous."}, {"start": 17.36, "end": 22.56, "text": "So being light or skinny doesn't mean being weak. There are three hidden reasons some"}, {"start": 22.56, "end": 27.48, "text": "skinny guys are freakishly strong. Two of them you can't see in the mirror, and the"}, {"start": 27.48, "end": 33.1, "text": "third is the one almost nobody trains. And at the end, there's a 12-week plan that needs nothing but"}, {"start": 33.1, "end": 42.06, "text": "a towel, a wall, and a bar. First, physics. How strong a muscle can be depends mostly on how"}, {"start": 42.06, "end": 48.24, "text": "thick it is, its cross-section. But your weight depends on your whole volume. Make a body twice"}, {"start": 48.24, "end": 54.56, "text": "as tall, and its muscles get about four times stronger, but it gets eight times heavier. That's"}, {"start": 54.56, "end": 59.98, "text": "why an ant can carry many times its own weight, and an elephant can't even jump. And it's why,"}, {"start": 60.38, "end": 65.62, "text": "in weightlifting, the lightest lifters lift the most for their size. Pound for pound,"}, {"start": 65.94, "end": 71.76, "text": "smaller bodies are stronger. Big guys still lift more in total, but for pull-ups, climbing,"}, {"start": 72.24, "end": 77.44, "text": "or anything where you move your own body, what counts is strength for your weight. And there,"}, {"start": 77.86, "end": 85.94, "text": "skinny is a head start. Second, your wiring. Strength isn't only muscle. It's how much of"}, {"start": 85.94, "end": 91.3, "text": "that muscle your brain can switch on at once. In one classic study, most of the strength people"}, {"start": 91.3, "end": 96.1, "text": "gained in their first four weeks of training came from their nervous system, not from bigger muscles."}, {"start": 96.82, "end": 102.12, "text": "Their brains learned to fire more of what was already there, so two arms the same size can"}, {"start": 102.12, "end": 107.22, "text": "have very different strength. One brain holds some of it back. The other has learned to fire"}, {"start": 107.22, "end": 115.7, "text": "much more of it at once. And third, the one almost nobody trains. Your tendons. Every bit"}, {"start": 115.7, "end": 121.16, "text": "of force your muscles make has to travel through them to reach your bones. Now picture lifting a"}, {"start": 121.16, "end": 126.66, "text": "weight with a rubber band, then with a steel cable. The rubber band stretches first, and the weight"}, {"start": 126.66, "end": 132.48, "text": "moves late. The cable moves it instantly. Stiffer tendons work like that cable, and studies link"}, {"start": 132.48, "end": 139.26, "text": "stiffer tendons to faster, more explosive force. And stiff doesn't mean tight or inflexible. It"}, {"start": 139.26, "end": 143.7, "text": "just means the tendon stretches less when it's pulled hard, so your force gets to the bone"}, {"start": 143.7, "end": 148.9, "text": "sooner. That's the snappy kind of strength you can't see in the mirror. But tendons are slow."}, {"start": 149.46, "end": 154.24, "text": "The core of your Achilles tendon is built while you're still growing, and scientists dating it"}, {"start": 154.24, "end": 159.56, "text": "with carbon from Cold War nuclear tests found it's barely replaced after about age 17."}, {"start": 162.17, "end": 168.09, "text": "So how do you train something that slow? Heavy. A big review of tendon studies found that loads"}, {"start": 168.09, "end": 174.07, "text": "above about 70% of your max change tendons a lot, while light training barely changed them at"}, {"start": 174.07, "end": 179.67, "text": "all. The type of exercise didn't matter much. Even simple holds worked, as long as they were hard."}, {"start": 180.33, "end": 184.77, "text": "Jumping was less effective, because each landing loads the tendon for only a split second."}, {"start": 185.39, "end": 189.97, "text": "That's why hundreds of easy push-ups can grow your muscles and still leave your tendons behind."}, {"start": 190.59, "end": 195.71, "text": "And if you're a teenager, this matters even more. Studies on young athletes found their muscles"}, {"start": 195.71, "end": 200.87, "text": "often get strong faster than their tendons can keep up, and that gap is one likely reason"}, {"start": 200.87, "end": 206.17, "text": "teens get tendon pain when they ramp up too fast. Piling on lots of jumping may be one of the"}, {"start": 206.17, "end": 213.39, "text": "riskier ways to do it. Here's how to train them at home, with no weights. Push or pull against"}, {"start": 213.39, "end": 219.27, "text": "something that can't move. One, stand on a towel, grab both ends, and pull up like a curl."}, {"start": 219.79, "end": 223.81, "text": "Two, plant your feet and push against a wall like you're trying to shove it over."}, {"start": 224.31, "end": 228.03, "text": "Three, hang from a bar and squeeze it like you're trying to crush it."}, {"start": 228.03, "end": 234.05, "text": "Push for five seconds, rest five, and do five reps. That's one set. The first week,"}, {"start": 234.43, "end": 239.23, "text": "go at about seven out of ten effort. After that, go as hard as you can without pain."}, {"start": 239.85, "end": 244.85, "text": "Do three sets of each, three times a week, and give it at least 12 weeks, because that's how"}, {"start": 244.85, "end": 251.05, "text": "long tendons take. Every two weeks, add a second to each hold, or pull the towel with one arm at"}, {"start": 251.05, "end": 256.21, "text": "a time. And before you start, time how long you can hang from the bar. Test it again in"}, {"start": 256.21, "end": 263.19, "text": "week 12. And one food tip. In a small study, gelatin with vitamin C an hour before exercise"}, {"start": 263.19, "end": 271.47, "text": "doubled a marker of collagen building. It's early evidence, but it's cheap. So the skinny guy in the"}, {"start": 271.47, "end": 278.07, "text": "anime isn't a lie. A light frame, a trained nervous system, and tendons like steel cables."}, {"start": 278.79, "end": 283.17, "text": "Muscle is what people see. Tendons are what they feel when you shake their hand."}], "words": [{"w": "In", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.24}, {"w": "1988,", "start": 0.24, "end": 0.84}, {"w": "a", "start": 1.4, "end": 1.48}, {"w": "man", "start": 1.48, "end": 1.68}, {"w": "who", "start": 1.68, "end": 1.92}, {"w": "weighed", "start": 1.92, "end": 2.06}, {"w": "60", "start": 2.06, "end": 2.54}, {"w": "kilos", "start": 2.54, "end": 2.96}, {"w": "lifted", "start": 2.96, "end": 3.5}, {"w": "190", "start": 3.5, "end": 4.24}, {"w": "kilos", "start": 4.24, "end": 5.1}, {"w": "over", "start": 5.1, "end": 5.48}, {"w": "his", "start": 5.48, "end": 5.7}, {"w": "head,", "start": 5.7, "end": 5.96}, {"w": "more", "start": 6.46, "end": 6.6}, {"w": "than", "start": 6.6, "end": 6.76}, {"w": "three", "start": 6.76, "end": 7.12}, {"w": "times", "start": 7.12, "end": 7.56}, {"w": "his", "start": 7.56, "end": 7.76}, {"w": "own", "start": 7.76, "end": 7.94}, {"w": "body", "start": 7.94, "end": 8.22}, {"w": "weight.", "start": 8.22, "end": 8.5}, {"w": "They", "start": 9.06, "end": 9.2}, {"w": "called", "start": 9.2, "end": 9.38}, {"w": "him", "start": 9.38, "end": 9.62}, {"w": "Pocket", "start": 9.62, "end": 10.08}, {"w": "Hercules.", "start": 10.08, "end": 10.6}, {"w": "It's", "start": 11.16, "end": 11.32}, {"w": "the", "start": 11.32, "end": 11.44}, {"w": "anime", "start": 11.44, "end": 11.7}, {"w": "trope", "start": 11.7, "end": 12.12}, {"w": "made", "start": 12.12, "end": 12.32}, {"w": "real.", "start": 12.32, "end": 12.62}, {"w": "The"